The settled principles are as follows :
"1. It cannot be laid down as an absolute rule of law that a dying declaration cannot form the sole basis of conviction unless it is corroborated;
CriAppeal-587-2002 2.

Each case must be determined on its own facts keeping in view the circumstances in which the dying declaration was made;
3. It cannot be laid down as a general proposition that a dying declaration is a weaker kind of evidence than other pieces of evidence;
4. A dying declaration stands on the same footing as another piece of evidence and has to be judged in the light of surrounding circumstances and with reference to the principles governing the weighing of evidence;
5. A dying declaration which has been recorded by a competent Magistrate in the proper manner, that is to say, in the form of questions and answers, and, as far as practicable, in tevidencehe words of the maker of the declaration, stands on a much higher footing than a dying declaration which depends upon oral testimony which may suffer from all the infirmities of human memory and human character : and Amrut Pundlik Marathe And Ors vs The State Of Maharashtra on 12 March, 2024 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/103136483/
